# Interop and unsafe code

The design for this is `docs/design.ja.md` §2.9, §2.11.1, §2.12. This rule is the set of promises reviews hold.

## Where unsafe code may live

- `unsafe`, function pointers, and `Unsafe`/`MemoryMarshal` reinterpretation are allowed only in `XylemUI.Platform.*`, `XylemUI.Rendering.*`, and the native wrappers of `XylemUI.Text`. `AllowUnsafeBlocks` is set in those projects only. Core stays safe.
- Every `unsafe` block has a comment that states the invariant that makes it safe (lifetime, length, alignment).
- A read or write through a pointer into mapped or native memory checks its index against that memory's size first. Out of range, a Release build ends with an access violation and a Debug build may pass by luck: in S7 `ReadPixel` read outside the back buffer, and only the Release test run crashed.

## Calling native code

- C APIs: `[LibraryImport]` with blittable signatures. No `DllImport` with runtime marshalling, no `string` marshalling on a frame path; pass UTF-16 or UTF-8 spans.
- Windows declarations come from TerraFX.Interop.Windows. COM is called through the vtable; no `ComImport` RCWs. Every `AddRef` has a matching `Release` in the same owner, and back-buffer views are released before `ResizeBuffers` (Invariant 11).
- A failed `HRESULT` becomes `InvalidOperationException` with the result in `HResult` (`COMException` is reserved by CA2201), and a lost device its own exception. Do not give a namespace a segment named like a TerraFX class: `XylemUI.Rendering.D3D11` hides TerraFX's `D3D11` class, so write `TerraFX.Interop.DirectX.D3D11` in full.
- A waitable object that counts (a DXGI frame latency object, a semaphore) is tested with a user that takes one and does not give it back. In S7 a frame that returned without `Present` left the swap chain's object unsignaled and no frame came again; the review found it, and the wait now has a timeout (design §2.11).
- A COM object that the OS calls back is a native struct whose first field points to a vtable of `[UnmanagedCallersOnly]` functions (`TextAnalysisSource` in `XylemUI.Platform.Windows`), not source-generated COM. In F1, `[GeneratedComClass]` with `StrategyBasedComWrappers` read the interface's attributes by reflection, which linked NativeAOT's reflection runtime and its metadata: `XylemUI.E2E.exe` was 4.71 MB instead of 4.28 MB (main 4.22 MB, PR for Issue #150). An object with many interfaces (the UIA providers of row P02, `IDropTarget`) is measured again in its row: hand-written vtables, or source-generated COM with a strategy that does not read attributes.
- On a path that must always end, such as the font fallback, an OS object of a kind the code cannot use is treated as absent, not thrown on. In F1 a font whose file loader is not local (a cloud font not yet downloaded) made `ResolveFont` throw, which would stop every split of a text; `DirectWriteFontProvider.PathOf` now returns `null` and the range gets the bundled font (PR #152 review).
- When a value an OS API returns selects the form of an output (a rendering mode, a texture type, a pixel format), every value its documentation allows is handled, first those a compared implementation handles. In T2 the ClearType path read DirectWrite's 3x1 texture for every rendering mode; an `ALIASED` mode has none, and the glyph would have had no ink. Skia pairs `ALIASED` with the 1x1 texture; the review found it (PR #183).
- A design that starts using an OS component writes what the component needs from the thread and the process: COM initialization and its apartment, the threads that may call it. WIC needs COM initialized on the calling thread, and the T6 design did not say who initializes it; the review found it, and the design then had the platform call `OleInitialize` on the UI thread, as GPUI, Avalonia, and Flutter do (PR #188). T6 later dropped WIC and the call with it; I1c's DirectManipulation brings it back, in `XylemApp.Run`, with `[STAThread]` on the app's `Main` (Issue #400); row PL1 moves the call into the Windows host, `Win32PlatformApp`, which `XylemApp.Run` makes before the window (Issue #908).
- The output of an OS call that converts a value (a key's scan code, a layout's character) is checked by a test that makes the real call before code relies on it; the documentation is not enough. In K1a `MapVirtualKey(..., MAPVK_VK_TO_VSC_EX)`, documented to put `0xE0` in the high byte of an extended key, gave `VK_LEFT` `0x4B` on Windows 11 26300, and a test with the real window read the arrow as `Numpad4` (PR #356).
- A host that starts to listen to an OS notification after the window is shown reads the current state once when it starts: the notification it missed does not come again. `DesktopWindow` wired the input after `Show`, so it never got the first `WM_ACTIVATE`, and was changed to read `GetActiveWindow` once (PR #356); since row PL1, the window core reads the host's `IsActive` when it connects.
- A design that depends on how an OS object counts or when it updates (a semaphore's bound, the moment frame statistics include a refresh) measures that behavior with a scratch build before the design PR, at the counts the product reaches. R3's design for presents that did not wait took the swap chain's frame latency object for an unbounded semaphore; it kept at most 40 signals, and a long resize drag then left the frames at about 16 a second. The frame statistics that the owner's chosen alternative read were a refresh behind when the object signaled, and that form waited at every frame (PRs #676 and #680).
- A value that a scratch build derives from OS counters is recorded with the counters it came from, and a derived value equal to the fallback is taken for the fallback until the counters show otherwise. Spike #687 read a refresh period of 16.6667 ms, the 60 Hz default; the refresh counts showed that the first count read (54,694,592) was larger than the later ones (about 54,609,187), the series having changed when the window moved to MPO, so the period was never derived. Anchoring again when the count went back gave 16.6662 ms (PR #691).
- A time extrapolated from the last OS sample (the next refresh from the last displayed one) states the longest gap it is extrapolated over, idle included, and the error at that gap. #691's design said that anchoring at each frame kept the period's error within one frame, which held only while animating; a key on a static screen comes minutes after the last present, and an error of 0.0005 ms a period grows to 18 ms in ten minutes. The design now draws at once when the last displayed refresh is more than one second old (PR #691's review).
- A window is made topmost after it is shown, and moved before. On Windows 11 26300 `SetWindowPos(HWND_TOPMOST)` on a hidden window returned success and left `WS_EX_TOPMOST` unset; a call whose result matters is checked by reading the state back. Moving the shown window cost about 3 ms (`MonitorChoice` in `XylemUI.E2E`, PR #664).
- A value read on a message that Windows sends to every top-level window (`WM_SETTINGCHANGE`) is kept per window, and each window compares what it reads with its own value. AN1's first `Win32Window.ReducesMotion` was static: the first window to get the message changed it, and the other windows saw no change and raised no event (PR #476).
- The scale of a window comes from `GetDpiForWindow`, not `GetDpiForSystem`: the system DPI stays the one of the sign-in when the display's scale changes after it. T4's text scene was changed to ask the window before the pictures at 100 % and 150 %, taken without signing in again (PR #173, row W1).
- In HLSL, the base of a `pow` whose exponent is not a constant is kept above 0 (`max(x, 1e-7)`). In T7's scratch shader `1.0 - pow(1.0 - c, e)` dropped every pixel of full coverage on this machine's GPU, and clamping the base fixed it (PR #196).
- macOS calls go through `objc_msgSend` P/Invoke. An Obj-C shim is added only where a block or a callback cannot be expressed in C#, and the PR says why.
- A value returned to the OS is made a default only after its documentation says what the OS does on receiving it. DD1's drop target returned `DROPEFFECT_MOVE` for a plain drop from the OS, as the key table of `IDropTarget::DragOver` gives; `DROPEFFECT_MOVE` tells the source to remove the data, so a plain drop from Explorer would have removed the files. The review found it, and the owner chose Copy, as GPUI does (PR #603).

## Callbacks from native code

- What the OS gives a window (a COM manager, a handle) is held by that window's object, not in a static field: an app has many windows. P02c's TSF first put its category and display attribute managers in static fields; a second window replaced them, and closing a window left the other's clauses without attributes or calling a released object (PR #540's review).
- A list of notices sent to the OS, which may call back while it is told, is taken out and emptied before it is sent. P02c's document sent its held text changes from the list itself; a lock the manager asked for while told one ended by sending the same list again (PR #540's review).
- A callback is a `static` method with `[UnmanagedCallersOnly]`. It catches every exception at its entry; a managed exception never crosses into native code (Invariant 13). An invariant failure still fails fast. A callback whose body only reads and writes through its pointer arguments, calls nothing that can throw (a constant such as a TerraFX IID, `Guid`'s `==`), and allocates nothing cannot throw a managed exception and has no `catch`, which would be a branch nothing reaches; its type says so in a comment (F1's `TextAnalysisSource`, PR #152's review).
- Native code never holds a managed object's address. It holds a generational id from `NativeHandleTable` (Invariant 8). No long-lived `GCHandle.Alloc(..., Pinned)`.
- A callback that arrives on a thread other than the UI thread does not touch Core; it posts to the UI queue (design §2.12).
- A window answers the messages of other processes from the moment it exists, before its content is made. A resident app on this machine sent `WM_GETOBJECT` while `DesktopWindow` showed its window, before it mounted the tree; AccessKit's activation read the tree's root, which did not exist yet, and the process ended at an invariant. CI has no such app, and only the local gate found it. A handler that such a message reaches answers that nothing is ready, and finishes once it is (PR #634).

## Native libraries

- HarfBuzz (every OS), AccessKit (Windows), and FreeType, SDL3, and wgpu-native (Linux) are linked statically in NativeAOT builds and loaded as shared libraries under the JIT, from the same `LibraryImport` declarations (design §9). Their versions are pinned in `vendor/versions.lock`. AccessKit is taken prebuilt from its release archive, not built; an app links it by importing `XylemUI.Platform.Windows.targets`, which also passes the seven system libraries it calls (PR for Issue #633).
- A static library linked into a NativeAOT executable is built with the static CRT (`/MT`): the NativeAOT runtime uses it, and a `/MD` static library fails the link with `LNK2038` (RuntimeLibrary mismatch). The DLL for the JIT uses `/MD` (S8a).

## Checks on a real machine

- While an IME composes, TSF takes every key, function keys included, and none reaches the window procedure. A check that must act during a composition (move the field, take the focus away) acts with the mouse. Spike #510 bound those actions to F2 and F5 for two runs, and neither reached the window (PR #511).
- A real machine's steps are written after asking the owner's keyboard layout, and they use only keys it has. P02c's reconversion step said to press the 変換 key; the owner's US keyboard has none, Space put a space in place of the selection, and a scratch tool that sent `VK_CONVERT` on F9 checked it again (PR #544).
- A check on a real machine names the IME (and the input method's version) that was used, read from the log or asked of the owner, not assumed. Spike #510 wrote Microsoft's Japanese IME; the owner had typed with Google Japanese Input, which a process list showed later (PR #515).
- A step that hands the owner's files or data to the OS (a drop from Explorer, a paste) is checked, before the steps are sent, for whether the OS may remove or move them, and it uses files made for the check. DD1's step dropped the owner's own files from Explorer on a build whose plain drop was still a move; the files were still there (PR #603).
